-- 122: SUPERGUN v1 DESIGN, specified against Gate N1 (seed 121) rather than against composed estimates.
-- Source doc: mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s9 (appended 2026-09-04). Follows seeds 117, 119, 120.
-- Method step 4 (tag and store). Lane numbering: SUPERGUN NETCODE takes EVEN numbers from 120 up.
-- Contains ONE CORRECTION to a Gate N1 presentation detail (the amortised cost column is eligibility-weighted, so it
-- understates the true per-frame worst case at large depths) and the design decisions taken from the corrected numbers.
-- Apply with tools/re_kb/apply_seed.py after the README export backup
-- (re_kb_data/_exports/re_kb_20260904-153645_pre122.surql). Idempotent and order-independent. No apostrophes in literals.
-- Do NOT re-run 07_dedup_edges.surql afterwards.
USE NS re DB kb;

UPSERT source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET kind='analysis', strength='reproduction', ref='mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s9; inputs are docs/RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE-N1.md (ENGINE lane, seed 121) and this lane measurements in d3dcap/net/sg_bench.cpp plus the d3dcap/receipt/runner timings recorded in seed 117', note='Design specification derived from measured numbers only. The arithmetic re-deriving the Gate N1 amortised column from its own per-event columns reproduces all eight depth rows to within p50-versus-mean noise, which is what establishes the eligibility-weighting correction rather than asserting it.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate_n1_amortisation_correction SET status='confirmed', text='CORRECTION to RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE-N1 s1.2 presentation, not to its result. The column labelled amortised ms/frame under the heading worst case, a rollback fired on every frame, is eligibility-weighted rather than per-frame worst case: a depth-N rollback is only eligible after tick k >= N, so a 200-tick run yields 200-N events and the column divides by all 200 frames. At depth 120 only 80 of 200 frames (40 percent) carried a rollback, which is why depths 60 and 120 read as equal (2.316 and 2.327 ms) while per-event resim goes 3.007 to 5.183 ms. Reconstructing the column as (200-N)/200 times (restore + resim) plus save reproduces all eight rows to within p50-versus-mean noise. THE NUMBER A DESIGN MUST BUDGET AGAINST IS THE PER-EVENT SUM save+restore+resim, because in a zero-delay scheme at a steady one-way delay of k frames a k-frame rollback fires on EVERY frame with no eligibility gap. Corrected per-event costs and share of the 16.667 ms budget: depth 2 = 0.072 ms (0.4 pct), 4 = 0.145 (0.9), 8 = 0.255 (1.5), 16 = 0.740 (4.4), 30 = 1.153 (6.9), 60 = 3.046 (18.3), 120 = 5.230 (31.4). The headline is UNCHANGED and if anything sharper: at the shipped depth of 8 the true worst case is 0.255 ms against 66.7 ms of frame delay. What changes is the CEILING: a 120-frame ring costs 31 percent of a frame, not 14, and a 60-frame ring costs 18 percent, not 14.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_save_set_specified SET status='confirmed', text='SUPERGUN v1 save set = blk[0..0x33B18) plus the GGPO counter at 0x142d10b90 (0x10 B) plus ctx[0x1F0000..0x200000) = 277,288 B. Gate N1 proved the MINIMUM sufficient set is blk + counter + eight bytes at ctx+0x1F8230..0x1F8238 (two f32, each 4-byte half required alone) = 211,760 B, and that the shipped blk-only set is INSUFFICIENT: it diverges from depth 2 up at blk+0x1D6FC = DC 0x8C27B034 = object-pool node 144 field +0x124, a sprite-walker placement field. We deliberately ship a WIDER set than the proven minimum because two of the Gate N1 caveats say the minimum is not known to be minimal elsewhere: it was measured on one stage-9 offline receipt, and the per-frame READER of those eight bytes is UNKNOWN (indexed access; no literal 0x1f8230 displacement exists in the 10,803-function disassembly cache, so the index can move). The Gate N1 bisection already proved the supersets PASS on this stage: 1F8000-1F9000 and 1F0000-200000. Cost of the insurance, from this lane measured copy scaling: 65,536 B = 0.0012 ms p50 per copy, so plus 0.04 ms per rollback event at a cap of 32 = plus 0.24 percent of a frame. Saving the whole 4 MB ctx (which also PASSed as simctx) would cost about 0.09 ms per save = about 2.9 ms per event at depth 32 and would force the cap down, so 64 KB is the right point on the curve.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_ring_rotation_rule SET status='confirmed', text='Ring rule derived from the Gate N1 cache-cliff attribution: restore p50 rises from 0.0045 ms at a 207 KB ring to about 0.024 ms at a 24 MB ring, about 5x, WHILE THE PER-EVENT COPY STAYS 211 KB, so the cliff is the ring WORKING SET and not the copy size. Therefore SUPERGUN allocates 65 slots (65 times 277,288 = 18.0 MB) so a deep recovery is always possible, but ROTATES MODULO active_cap+1 rather than modulo the allocated count. A player on a clean 40 ms link runs active_cap 4, touches five slots = 1.4 MB, and never pays the cliff; a player on a bad link pays it only while the link is bad. At the default cap of 32 the working set is 33 times 277,288 = 9.15 MB, just past the Gate N1 depth-30 point (6.3 MB, restore 0.0185 ms).';

UPSERT finding:supergun_speculation_cap_specified SET status='inferred', text='SUPERGUN v1 speculation cap: 64 frames allocated (1.07 s of staleness), 32 frames default ACTIVE (533 ms), adaptive as clamp(ceil(owd_p99) + jitter_margin, 4, 32). Per-event cost at 32 is about 1.2 ms = 7.4 percent of the 16.667 ms budget, interpolating the corrected Gate N1 depths 16 and 30. Overrun policy: extend toward 64 and spend up to about 5.2 ms on that frame RATHER THAN STALL, because a stall costs 16.7 ms and spending 31 percent of a frame to avoid one is unambiguously right. Beyond 64 rollback cannot recover and the session needs a state resync (unproven, see finding:supergun_resync_unproven). TAGGED INFERRED AND NOT CONFIRMED FOR ONE REASON: Gate N1 reports p50 only, and a frame budget is a DEADLINE not an average. This lane measured tick p99/p50 ratios of 2.2x idle and 4.0x on real inputs; applied to the Gate N1 depth-30 resim that is a p99 of 2.3 to 4.6 ms, comfortable at a cap of 32, but applied at depth 120 it is 11 to 21 ms, i.e. a p99 rollback at the hard cap can MISS THE FRAME DEADLINE OUTRIGHT. The cap of 32 is chosen to carry that headroom and is PROVISIONAL until Gate N1b lands.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_frame_delay_policy SET status='confirmed', text='SUPERGUN frame delay policy: default d = 0, per-player, user-adjustable 0 to 4. The semantics are CONFIRMED from upstream GGPO source (input_queue.cpp AdvanceQueueHead does frame += _frame_delay): frame delay is applied to the LOCAL player own input before it enters the sim, so it is a SELF-IMPOSED LATENCY THAT BUYS THE OPPONENT PREDICTION ACCURACY. My delay costs me latency and saves my opponent from predicting me. It is therefore correctly a per-player setting, and a player on a poor uplink can voluntarily add delay to stop their opponent seeing corrections. THE HONEST COST, which the first draft of s4 understated: d = 0 does not merely remove 66.7 ms, it also MAXIMISES CORRECTION FREQUENCY. At a steady one-way delay of k frames every frame carries a k-frame rollback, whereas at d = k most frames carry none. The CPU is free; the visual correction rate is not. That is why d stays user-selectable and why Gate N2 (prediction quality measured as VISIBLE STATE DELTA, not bit accuracy) is the gate that decides how far zero delay can be pushed. Also specified: no TIMESYNC Sleep (continuous adjustment of at most 1 percent of the frame period, 0.17 ms, expressible at the measured 10 microsecond spin-to-deadline precision) and all send and receive timestamps taken from QPC directly, never from the frame-latched clock that seed 119 found the shipped build using.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_desync_hash_rule SET status='confirmed', text='Desync detection rule forced by Gate N1 s2: the tick itself is NONDETERMINISTIC in two ctx buckets (decoded texture pages ctx+0x100030..0x108FC0 and page-table records ctx+0x108FC0..0x1E0030) even with everything restored, and neither is on the frame read set. Therefore SUPERGUN hashes THE SAVE SET AND NOTHING ELSE, never ctx outside 0x1F0000..0x200000; a detector that hashed render scratch would fire constantly and train everyone to ignore it. Cost, scaling this lane measured FNV-1a of 0.0248 ms over 211,736 B to 277,288 B: about 0.033 ms = 0.2 percent of a frame, so hashing every frame locally is affordable and the hash is exchanged with the peer every 30 frames. Per Gate N1, any ctx bucket OTHER than those two differing is a real failure, especially the texture-slot table which the tick reads as well as writes.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_resync_unproven SET status='open', text='OPEN. Beyond the 64-frame ring, rollback cannot recover and the session must resync from a state snapshot. The state is 277,288 B, roughly 22 ms of wire on a 100 Mbit link, so unlike GGPO which can only freeze, we could in principle just resend it. THIS IS DESIGNED, NOT PROVEN, AND THERE IS A SPECIFIC REASON TO DOUBT IT: per the rr-ggpo-determinism findings a BATTLE-state blk is pointer-rich (557 pointers into the decompressed per-character asset image) and restoring one into a different process KILLED THE GAME TWICE; only character-select states were shown portable. Same-build same-content peers make it more tractable, and the receipt runner Delta = 0 arena placement removes relocation entirely when both sides map at the same base, but none of that is measured. Until Gate N5b passes, the honest fallback beyond 64 frames is a STALL, not a resync.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate_n1b_needed SET status='open', text='GATE N1b, requested of the ENGINE lane by the netcode lane: re-emit the RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE-N1 s1.2 cost table with p99 and max columns for save, restore and resim at every depth, plus a count of rollback events whose total exceeded 16.667 ms. A frame budget is a deadline, not an average, and the entire table is currently p50. This lane measured tick p99/p50 ratios of 2.2x idle and 4.0x on real inputs; at depth 120 that projects a p99 rollback of 11 to 21 ms, which would miss the frame outright. The SUPERGUN default speculation cap of 32 is PROVISIONAL until this lands, and the hard cap of 64 is set from it.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate_n1c_needed SET status='open', text='GATE N1c, requested of the ENGINE lane for Gate 4: re-run the Gate N1 s3 ctx save-set ladder on a DIFFERENT stage, camera and a HUD-heavy frame. Gate N1 measured the required ctx window on one stage-9 offline receipt only, and the per-frame reader of those bytes is UNKNOWN because the access is indexed. This FALSIFIES the SUPERGUN save-set decision if any required byte falls outside ctx[0x1F0000..0x200000): the window must then widen. If required bytes turn out to be scattered across ctx, the save set becomes the whole 4 MB, the speculation cap must drop to about 8, and the DEEP-speculation thesis is dead, though zero frame delay at shallow depth would still beat the shipped 66.7 ms. This is the single largest technical risk in the SUPERGUN design.';

-- edges
RELATE finding:supergun_gate_n1_amortisation_correction->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_gate_n1_amort_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_save_set_specified->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_save_set_specified_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_ring_rotation_rule->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_ring_rotation_rule_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_speculation_cap_specified->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_speculation_cap_specified_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_frame_delay_policy->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_frame_delay_policy_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_desync_hash_rule->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_desync_hash_rule_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_resync_unproven->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_resync_unproven_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_gate_n1b_needed->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_gate_n1b_needed_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_gate_n1c_needed->cites->source:supergun_design_v1_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_gate_n1c_needed_src;

-- the seed-117 composed speculation-budget estimate is superseded by the measured interleaved numbers
UPDATE finding:supergun_speculation_budget SET status='superseded', note='SUPERSEDED 2026-09-04 by Gate N1 (seed 121) and its corrected per-event reading (seed 122, finding:supergun_gate_n1_amortisation_correction). The seed-117 figures were DERIVED by composing a tick measurement with a separately measured memcpy; Gate N1 measured the interleaved loop directly. The composed model was close at shallow depths (predicted 0.34 ms at depth 8 versus 0.255 ms measured) but it could say NOTHING about sufficiency, and sufficiency is exactly where the assumption turned out to be false: the shipped blk-only save set is insufficient for our runner. Use the measured per-event table, not this estimate.';
